{
  "headline": "President Mahama states listing 10 state-owned enterprises on stock exchange will reduce political interference",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Speaking at the Council on Foreign Relations in New York on September 25, 2026, President John Dramani Mahama stated that the intended listing of 10 state-owned enterprises (SOEs) on the Ghana Stock Exchange (GSE) would transform them into public companies. According to the President, this move is intended to enforce accountability and reduce political interference in the management of these enterprises. He described the plan as part of reforms aimed at improving governance and profitability while attracting investment into the SOEs. The remarks were made during a session titled “A Conversation with President John Dramani Mahama,” which focused on Ghana's energy access and investment climate."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "U.S. import bans on Canadian alcohol, dairy, motorcycles, and molasses take effect",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "As of 12:01 a.m. ET on September 28, 2026, the United States has implemented import bans on several Canadian products, including alcohol, dairy byproducts such as whey, motorcycles, and molasses. The measures, announced via executive orders signed by President Donald Trump on September 8, follow Canada's implementation of tariffs on various U.S. goods earlier this month. Trump stated the bans are a response to what he described as continued discrimination by Canada regarding U.S. dairy, automotives, and alcohol, accusing Canada of treating the United States unfairly."
    },
    {
      "text": "The ban on liquor applies to nearly C$1bn ($710m; £530m) worth of Canadian alcohol exported to the U.S. According to BBC World, approximately 90% of all Canadian alcohol exports were bound for the U.S. in 2025, with most originating from Ontario. Spirits producers may face significant impacts because Canada exports more liquor to the U.S. than beer or wine. The motorcycle ban affects exports valued at approximately C$120m, based on Statistics Canada data showing about 5,000 motorcycles were sent to the U.S. in 2025. Robert Glasgow, an international trade lawyer with KPMG Law, noted that while the macro impact may not be massive, individual business owners stand to be hurt by the stoppage of alcohol products. For example, James Lester, founder of the B.C.-based Sons of Vancouver distillery, reported that while U.S. shipments represent less than 10% of his business, the ban shuts his products out of the U.S. market entirely."
    },
    {
      "text": "Canadian Prime Minister Mark Carney stated earlier this month that the impact of the import bans on the Canadian economy would be modest, describing them as relatively modest measures compared to other U.S. trade actions. U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer told CNBC that President Trump is comfortable with the current relationship with Canada, adding that while there are conversations regarding potential deals, there is no urgency on the U.S. side. Trade talks currently remain stalled."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "National Rally leader Jordan Bardella claims 'total war' against party following antisemitism allegations",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Jordan Bardella, head of the far-right National Rally (RN), told party lawmakers and senators at a parliamentary meeting on Tuesday that a “total war” is being waged against his party. Speaking to a standing ovation, Bardella characterized the claims as an attempt to destabilize the presidential campaign and damage his party as Marine Le Pen leads polls ahead of the 2027 presidential election. He accused the investigative website Mediapart of attempting “to kill my political career” and stated he would pursue legal action for defamation, maintaining that the report is false. Marine Le Pen expressed “absolute confidence” in Bardella during the meeting. While political opponents have called for Bardella's resignation, the allegations stem from a Mediapart report regarding comments he allegedly wrote between 2013 and 2015 while aged 17 to 18 and a member of the Front National."
    },
    {
      "text": "Mediapart journalists stated they independently authenticated the alleged comments, which were reportedly made via messaging services and email groups. The allegations include claims that “the Jew has to dominate other peoples, crush them, and rob them; a Zionist is necessarily a Jew, and fundamentally so,” as well as the assertion that “all the banks are owned by Jews.” The investigation follows Le Pen’s efforts since 2011 to move the party away from its antisemitic past to increase electoral reach."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Vessel struck by unknown projectile in Strait of Hormuz, UKMTO says",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The United Kingdom Maritime Trade Operations (UKMTO) reported that a vessel was struck by an unknown projectile while transiting the Strait of Hormuz on the evening of September 28, 2026. According to UKMTO, the strike caused a fire on board, but the fire was extinguished and all crew members were reported safe. The agency cited a delayed report from a third party regarding the incident. UKMTO did not identify the vessel, the origin of the projectile, or the party responsible for the strike."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ghana and Saint Kitts and Nevis sign bilateral labour agreement for Ghanaian nurses",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Ghana and Saint Kitts and Nevis have signed a bilateral labour agreement intended to improve conditions for Ghanaian nurses employed in the Caribbean nation. According to Foreign Affairs Minister Samuel Okudzeto Ablakwa, the agreement provides legally protected enhanced salaries, health insurance, rent allowance, end-of-service benefits, and other labour rights for nurses sent to work in Saint Kitts and Nevis. The agreement was signed by Minister Ablakwa and Saint Kitts and Nevis Deputy Prime Minister Dr. Geoffrey Hanley on the sidelines of the 81st Session of the United Nations General Assembly in New York. In a Facebook post on Tuesday, Mr. Ablakwa stated the agreement reflects a continued demand for the expertise of Ghanaian professionals abroad and noted that Ghana would continue to pursue diplomacy that delivers benefits to its citizens."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Syria to allow non-exclusive agencies for foreign vehicle, machinery, and tire companies",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Syrian Ministry of Economy and Industry announced Tuesday that the country will allow foreign vehicle, machinery, and tire companies to register non-exclusive agencies and open branches or offices within Syria. According to a decision published on Telegram, these companies must register with the Foreign Companies Branches and Agencies Department at the General Directorate of Internal Trade and Consumer Protection in accordance with existing regulations. The ministry has not provided a timetable for when registrations are scheduled to begin. This development follows a September 2025 move in which the ministry transferred the department responsible for foreign company branches and agencies from its foreign trade directorate to the General Directorate of Internal Trade and Consumer Protection in Damascus. The ministry stated the transfer was intended to streamline administration and strengthen oversight of foreign companies operating in the country."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "KNUST and UNCDF host EU Ambassador to advance skills development initiative",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Career Services Centre of the Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology (KNUST) has reaffirmed its commitment to providing students with industry-ready skills through collaborations with the United Nations Capital Development Fund (UNCDF) and the European Union. The European Union Ambassador to Ghana, H.E. Rune Skinnebach, and a delegation visited the university’s Career Services Centre to focus on youth empowerment, employability, digital skills training, and academic-industry collaboration. The visit aimed to strengthen cooperation among KNUST, the European Union, and UNCDF to advance innovation, entrepreneurship, and youth employability in Ghana and beyond. Ambassador Skinnebach described KNUST as a leading force in higher education and innovation within Ghana and West Africa, noting the European Union's commitment to supporting young people through initiatives such as Erasmus+ and European Horizons."
    },
    {
      "text": "Mrs. Selina Angmor Doe, Director of the KNUST Career Services Centre, stated that the university was selected last year as an implementer of the European Union’s Skills Project in collaboration with UNCDF. According to Doe, the partnership has resulted in the reconstruction of the Centre’s website to include a job board for employers to upload opportunities for students and graduates. Additionally, the partnership has enabled the training of over 3,000 students in IT skills intended to increase international competitiveness."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ontario woman acquitted in 2002 infant death following new medical evidence",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Jennifer Gaskin was acquitted on Tuesday in the death of her infant son, Joeseph, after more than two decades of being labeled as his killer. Gaskin was originally convicted in 2002 after two medical experts testified that her 2 1/2-month-old son had died as a result of shaken baby syndrome. Joeseph was a premature baby who had spent the first two months of his life in a hospital. Following an Ontario provincial examination of shaken-baby cases occurring between the mid-1980s and mid-2000s, Gaskin's legal team retained three additional experts to review the case file. During court proceedings on Tuesday, these three experts stated that while they could not rule out the possibility of an inflicted injury, Joeseph could also have died from sudden infant death syndrome. The Crown informed the Appeal Court that the original expert evidence supporting the conviction had been 'wholly undermined and placed in grave doubt,' and requested that the new findings be admitted as fresh evidence to allow for Gaskin's acquittal."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Japan removes 16 Syrian entities from asset-freezing sanctions list",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Japan's Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced on Tuesday that it has removed 16 Syrian entities from its list of organizations subject to asset-freezing and other sanctions. The decision, which took effect through a Foreign Ministry notice, follows changes in the situation in Syria and Tokyo's consideration of bilateral relations with Damascus. Japan has maintained asset-freezing and other measures against Bashar al-Assad, as well as individuals and entities linked to his government, since 2011. Following the collapse of the Assad regime in December 2024, Japan partially eased these measures in May 2025, aligning with steps taken by other major countries. According to the Ministry, the latest decision was based on a Cabinet understanding regarding the partial lifting of sanctions and reflects subsequent developments in Syria."
    },
    {
      "text": "The 16 entities included in the lifting of measures are Syriatel, the Military Housing Establishment, Real Estate Bank, the Political Security Directorate, Commercial Bank of Syria, Syrian Lebanese Commercial Bank, Syria Trading Oil Company (Sytrol), General Petroleum Corporation, Syrian Petroleum Company, and the Syrian Company for Oil Transport. Also removed from the list are the General Organization of Radio and TV, Syria International Islamic Bank, the Ministries of Defense and Interior, the Cotton Marketing Organization, and Mahrukat Company, which manages the storage and distribution of petroleum products. The Foreign Ministry stated that the broader sanctions regime will not be fully removed; asset-freezing and related measures will remain in place against 59 individuals and 15 entities."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Estonia attributes August arson attack on drone manufacturer to Russian sabotage",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Estonia has stated that an August 15 arson attack on a building in Tallinn used by Milrem Robotics was an act of sabotage commissioned by the special services of the Russian Federation. The manufacturer produces unmanned ground vehicles used in Ukraine. Estonian Foreign Minister Margus Tsahkna said on Tuesday that authorities have concluded with certainty that the attack was part of a broader campaign across Europe intended to intimidate Estonia and weaken support for Ukraine. Following the announcement, Tsahkna stated that Russia’s chargé d’affaires in Tallinn was being summoned to his ministry and described the attacks as “absolutely unacceptable.” An Estonian national security agency reported that two Latvian suspects were detained in Latvia on August 17, followed by a third suspect on August 18. All three individuals were subsequently handed over to Estonia. Kremlin spokesperson Dmitry Peskov rejected the allegations, calling them “unfounded, ill-considered, absolutely not reflecting the reality, not containing any substantiation whatsoever,” and stated that Russia would not take the statements seriously. European and NATO leaders, including NATO chief Mark Rutte, expressed support for Estonia and Ukraine, with Rutte stating that Russia is attempting to weaken resolve and stop support for Ukraine. Estonian Prime Minister Kristen Michal stated the country's response would involve stronger domestic security, more support for Ukraine, and greater pressure on Russia."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Toronto mayoral candidates clash over use of AI in campaigns",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Toronto mayoral candidate Brad Bradford is defending his campaign's use of an artificial intelligence app following criticism from other candidates. According to CBC Toronto, the controversy arose after a National Observer report alleged that Bradford's campaign uses an AI app to provide online talking points to supporters. That report also alleged the app rewards users for sharing phone contacts with the campaign and posting social media comments. On September 28, 2026, Bradford denied that users receive any rewards, such as facetime, for posting AI-generated messages. His team stated to CBC News that the app's messaging and suggested comment features are intended to help supporters share their views with their networks. Bradford compared the use of the app to the campaign app used by Olivia Chow, stating the goal is to connect with voters. While technology experts noted that AI could serve as an equalizer for under-resourced teams, they stated it is no substitute for the human element of traditional voter persuasion."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Putin signs decree increasing authorized size of Russian Armed Forces to 2.44 million",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Russian President Vladimir Putin signed a decree on Tuesday increasing the authorized strength of the Russian Armed Forces to 2,441,630 personnel, which includes 1,550,500 service members, according to SANA English. The decree, which took effect upon signing, was published on Russia’s official legal information portal. The order instructs the Russian government to allocate necessary federal budget funding to the Defense Ministry to implement the provisions. This increase is linked to the creation of military construction units."
    },
    {
      "text": "The new decree repeals Decree No. 419, which was issued on June 12 and previously set the authorized strength of the armed forces. This follows a previous increase in July that brought the total authorized strength to 2.426 million personnel, including 1.535 million service members."
    }
  ]
}
